# Analyzers — the curated set whose TAGS feed case fusion.
#
# Every analyzer here runs automatically on each imported timeline, and the
# fusion layer selects events by tag existence — so this list IS the
# "selective analyzers" control. Curated for signal-per-event on a KAPE
# timeline; measured 2026-08-27 on a real 380k-event _BasicCollection import.
#
# Dropped from the old 15 on purpose (do not re-add casually):
#   chain, similarity_scorer, sessionizer — session/similarity tag floods with
#     no detection value for fusion; they made "has a tag" meaningless.
#   feature_extraction — writes attributes, not detection tags.
#
# BUT feature_extraction COMES BACK REGARDLESS, and removing it from this list
# does not stop it. Timesketch expands AUTO_SKETCH_ANALYZERS through
# analyzers/manager.py:_build_dependencies, and both `domain` and
# `account_finder` declare DEPENDENCIES = frozenset(["feature_extraction"]).
# Measured on a 4.16M-event timeline: 44 of the 72 scheduled sessions are
# feature_extraction, pulled in by `domain`. Keeping `domain` is the right
# trade — `rare-domain` was one of only four tags a real Windows timeline
# produced, and it is a genuine detection — but be honest about the cost: this
# curation removes 3 sessions, not 47. Dropping feature_extraction for real
# means dropping `domain` and `account_finder` too.
# The workflow waits for this set to finish before the run goes terminal
# (services/timesketch_service.wait_for_analyzers), so every analyzer added
# here also lengthens the pipeline's tail.
# `sigma` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. Timesketch ships an empty sigmarule table and
# that is not an oversight to fix: plaso's EVTX parser emits event_identifier, a
# POSITIONAL strings[] array and xml_string — and no named Windows fields at
# all. Sigma rules match on names (Image, CommandLine, ParentImage,
# OriginalFileName, TargetUserName). Measured against 79,019 real attack events
# covering every ATT&CK tactic, with 53 stable SigmaHQ Windows rules loaded:
# 53 analyzer sessions ran DONE and produced ZERO tags, because 0 of the 53
# rules can be satisfied by fields plaso does not emit. It also costs one
# analyzer session PER RULE per timeline.
#
# Sigma detection on this appliance lives in the VELOCIRAPTOR path instead
# (Hayabusa parses EVTX into named fields properly), which is why the two
# modules are complementary rather than redundant.
# Timesketch's own auto-hook runs these, which is the right place for them:
# it also covers timelines an analyst uploads through the Timesketch GUI, not
# just the ones this appliance imports.
#
# Timesketch's upload path calls build_sketch_analysis_pipeline WITHOUT a
# timeline_id (lib/tasks.py:471); the parameter defaults to None while its
# other caller, the add-timeline API (timeline.py:206), passes it correctly.
# So every analyzer this list would auto-run receives timeline_id=None, and
# that one omission produces two failures that look unrelated:
#
#   * the analyzer's own output fails jsonschema ("None is not of type
#     'integer'" on platform_meta_data.timeline_id), so it reports ERROR after
#     having tagged its events perfectly well;
#   * saved aggregations are written with "timeline_ids": [null], so every
#     visualization the analyzers create fails to open with
#     RequestError(400, 'No value specified for terms query').
#
# Verified side by side on one sketch: aggregations from an explicitly
# scheduled run carry "timeline_ids": [6] and work; auto-scheduled ones carry
# [null] and do not.
#
# THE ROOT CAUSE IS PATCHED, not worked around: analyzer_patches/apply.sh
# adds the missing `timeline_id=timeline_id` to that call
# (INTACT-PATCH pass-timeline-id), so the auto-hook now schedules correctly for
# every caller. Without that patch this list must stay EMPTY — running these
# with timeline_id=None is worse than not running them, because they report
# ERROR while silently writing visualizations nobody can open.
# 26 of the 35 analyzers this Timesketch ships. Every one here was RUN against
# real data and observed to complete; the nine that are absent are absent for a
# measured reason, not caution:
#
#   feature_extraction  runs anyway — `domain` and `account_finder` declare it
#                       as a DEPENDENCY (analyzers/manager.py:_build_dependencies),
#                       so listing it here would only duplicate it.
#   sigma               0 of 53 stable SigmaHQ Windows rules are satisfiable on
#                       plaso data: plaso's EVTX parser emits event_identifier,
#                       a positional strings[] and xml_string, and NO named
#                       fields (Image, CommandLine, ParentImage) — which is what
#                       sigma matches on. Measured on 79,019 real attack events:
#                       53 sessions DONE, ZERO tags. It is also one session PER
#                       RULE per timeline. Sigma detection lives in the
#                       Velociraptor/Hayabusa path, which parses EVTX properly.
#   hashr_lookup        refuses to start without a hashR database ("please
#                       uncomment the section and provide the connection
#                       details"). Add the config, then add it here.
#   yeti* (6)           never leave PENDING without a Yeti API root and key —
#                       not an error, they simply never start, so they would
#                       hold every import open. Configure Yeti, then add them.
#                       (wait_for_analyzers stalls out after 15 min regardless,
#                       so a stuck analyzer can no longer block a pipeline.)
#
# The enrichment analyzers near the bottom run clean with no service
# configured — they are harmless no-ops that start producing the moment their
# credentials exist, which is why they are on rather than waiting for a
# config change nobody will remember to make.
